  lead: 'Use this <strong>watts to volts calculator</strong> to convert electrical power into voltage when you know the current. The core relationship is V = W ÷ A, derived directly from the power formula P = V × I. Enter watts and amps to get volts instantly, with kilowatt support and an AC power-factor note.',
  reviewed: 'September 5, 2026',
  featureList: ['Convert watts to volts with V = W ÷ A', 'Handles watts and kilowatts', 'AC power-factor guidance for real loads', 'Works for DC and single-phase AC circuits'],
  methodology: 'The calculator rearranges the DC power equation P = V × I into V = P ÷ I to solve for voltage. Power entered in watts is divided by current in amps to return volts. For AC circuits the tool notes that measured (true) power already includes power factor, so the same division yields the RMS supply voltage; a separate kW figure is shown for convenience.',
